 bit gets  reflected and so the second rainbow is  always much fainter than the first so it  is actually always there and in fact  very occasionally you can even see a  third rainbow which is even fainter  still so these other rainbows are always  still there but they're always much  fainter so if you can only just see the  primary rainbow you're not going to see  the secondary rainbow at all and it's  always when you have a really stonkingly  bright primary rainbow that you end up  seeing the secondary one people often  ask this question  you know don't you lose the magic of  something by actually understanding it  you know by actually understanding where  a rainbow comes from isn't it not  beautiful anymore because suddenly  you've explained it and it's all very  simple and trivial  and of course it doesn't right i mean  you know i understand the physics of  rainbows but i can still go out and look  at one and say wow that's pretty so it  really did you know it doesn't have that  negative effect at all it's just it just  adds to the experience of seeing a  rainbow that not only does it look  beautiful but you actually understand  why it looks beautiful too

